    "created_at": "2018-05-10T20:46:04Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-05-10T20:46:04Z",
    "body": "In terms of Gecko's implementation, looks to me like we use the document's referrer policy for `<script>` loads at the moment.  But all that stuff is threaded through the whole machinery, so supporting a referrerpolicy on `<script>` would be quite straightforward:\r\n\r\n1. Adjust `nsHtml5TreeOpExecutor::PreloadScript` to look more like `nsHtml5TreeOpExecutor::PreloadStyle` in terms of referrer policy handling.\r\n2. Modify `ScriptLoader::LookupPreloadRequest` to do a slightly different referrer policy comparison (taking the element policy into account).\r\n3. Modify `ScriptLoader::ProcessExternalScript` in the \"no matching preload\" case to get the referrer policy from the element if it's set.  Needs a tiny bit of plumbing to expose it there,  but pretty simple.\r\n\r\nI don't know whether inline scripts need a concept of  referrer policy or whether it's even observable if they have one.  I suspect it's not.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/html/issues/comments/388180369/reactions",
